      "title": "The Silk Road",
      "body": "The Silk Road was never a single road, nor was it used only for silk. It was a shifting web of routes that linked China to the Mediterranean, crossing deserts, mountains, and the territories of many peoples.\n\nGoods certainly moved along it — silk travelling west, but also glass, spices, and horses in many directions. Yet historians increasingly stress that ideas travelled just as far. Religions, technologies, and artistic styles spread between distant cultures, carried by the very merchants who carried cloth.\n\nThe network declined as sea routes grew cheaper and safer from the fifteenth century onward. Even so, its lasting importance lies less in the trade itself than in what that trade made possible: sustained contact between civilisations that might otherwise have remained strangers.",
